 question:'Does "an executed exposure" in my D56 required change mean the last executed exposure of the week, as R7A1-FREEZE-A1 implements, or any executed exposure, and does R7A1-FREEZE-A1 meet the final formulation for the regain cap and the return cap?',
 facts:['Minimal timeline (my S1 section): constant 25 minutes: 2x5 re-establishment, then 2x5 trimmed from 3 each week with the regain cap held at 3; budget raised to 75 after week 8: 3x5, then 4x5 (cap ended), then 3x5 in consolidation; R7A-FREEZE-2 gave 2x5 then 4x5.','QE-R7A-22 setup (my section, bench press on days 0 and 3, 75 minutes): day 0 executed 3x5 whole, a systemic fatigue report on Tuesday evening reduced day 3 to 2x5 @RIR4; the regain cap stays 3 on both runtimes; "any exposure" would release it.','Legacy representation: week-8 rows without counts read the week’s planned counts; the review keeps the same cap (3) as with counts.','My restoration grid (864 timelines, ssctr-r7a1-grid.mjs): no regain or return cap rises after a week whose last executed exposure was not whole (A, rises only); 1,504 cap-weeks with a time-trimmed last exposure covered (923 regain, 581 return). Return-cap ends: 20 after a time-trimmed last exposure, 8 of them after weeks whose every exposure was fitted below; 38 counts two sets above the largest count executed since a return (1 to 3), all RUNNING_SUPPORT at 20 minutes after 16-24 day returns, from the cap ending against the ending week’s normal count while the same transition adds the block-boundary set (traced; the same mechanism on R7A-FREEZE-2, where the grid counts 174 such jumps and 307 rises or ends after trimmed weeks).','"Last" against the combined reading: in 517 cap-weeks an earlier exposure that week was whole and the last was time-trimmed; the cap holds (451 exercises still capped at week 15 under a constant budget).'],
 unknowns:['No study compares a last-session with an any-session rule for restoring a set count.','How often a persistent tight budget trims the last session of the week while an earlier one fits the whole count is unknown.'],
